# Binaries and build output
/bin/
/dist/
*.exe
*.test
*.out

# Environment / secrets — never committed (spec §11)
.env
.env.*
# ...except the committed template operators copy to their real .env
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Key material. The master key is escrowed OFFLINE (ADR-005, docs/key-escrow.md)
# and the backup artifact's age identity lives with whoever restores — neither
# ever belongs in the repo. These patterns catch the throwaway key material the
# restore drill generates, and act as a backstop against a real one being staged
# by accident.
*.age
*.key
age-identity*
backup-identity*
recovery-key*

# Scratch dirs from scripts/backup.sh and scripts/restore-drill.sh (they are
# PID-scoped and self-cleaning; this catches the debris of an interrupted run,
# which can contain a throwaway master key and a decrypted payload).
.backup-tmp.*
.drill-tmp.*
# The same for the three end-to-end lanes (scripts/a11y-e2e.sh,
# hostile-content-e2e.sh, inline-images-e2e.sh). Also PID-scoped and
# self-cleaning, and also worth a backstop: each one generates a throwaway
# Dovecot TLS key inside its scratch dir, and a cancelled run (SIGTERM before
# the trap, mailroom#346) leaves it on disk next to a tree somebody is about to
# `git add -A`. `.a11y-e2e-shots` is where #514's rendering check drops its
# screenshots — evidence for a review, never a tracked file.
.a11y-e2e-tmp.*
.a11y-e2e-shots/
.hostile-e2e-tmp.*
.inline-e2e-tmp.*

# Editor noise
.idea/
.vscode/

# Local tool state
.claude/
